[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Value Types vs Reference Types: Structs, Classes, and Copy-on-Write"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding the distinction between value types and reference types is fundamental to writing performant, bug-free iOS applications in Swift. In this tutorial, we will explore structs, classes, memory management, and how Swift optimizes performance using Copy-on-Write."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts: Structs vs Classes"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Swift, structs are value types, while classes are reference types. This fundamental difference dictates how data is stored, passed, and modified across your application."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Value Types (Structs): Each instance keeps a unique copy of its data. When assigned to a new variable or passed to a function, the entire value is copied."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference Types (Classes): Instances share a single copy of their data. Multiple variables point to the same memory location (heap), meaning changes affect all references."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"struct Point {
    var x: Int
    var y: Int
}

class Profile {
    var username: String
    init(username: String) {
        self.username = username
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Memory Layout: Stack vs Heap"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Value types are typically allocated on the stack (unless they capture reference types or live inside closures/classes), which makes allocation and deallocation extremely fast. Reference types store their instance data on the heap, requiring reference counting (ARC) overhead to manage lifetimes."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Copy-on-Write (CoW)"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Copy-on-Write is an optimization technique used by Swift's standard library collections like Array, Dictionary, and Set. Because copying large arrays eagerly would destroy performance, Swift delays the actual memory copy until the data is modified."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"var array1 = [1, 2, 3]
var array2 = array1 // No memory copy yet! Both point to the same buffer.

array2.append(4) // Copy-on-Write triggers here. A new buffer is allocated for array2."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Custom Copy-on-Write"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can implement your own Copy-on-Write behavior for custom structs that wrap heavy reference types by using Swift's isKnownUniquelyReferenced function."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"final class Storage {
    var data: [Int]
    init(data: [Int]) {
        self.data = data
    }
}

struct OptimizedBuffer {
    private var storage: Storage

    init(data: [Int]) {
        self.storage = Storage(data: data)
    }

    var data: [Int] {
        get { return storage.data }
        set {
            if !isKnownUniquelyReferenced(&storage) {
                storage = Storage(data: newValue)
            } else {
                storage.data = newValue
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Best Practices"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Default to structs for data models, UI state, and immutable structures."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use classes when you specifically need reference semantics, inheritance, or Objective-C interoperability."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage Copy-on-Write patterns when designing custom heavy data structures to combine value type safety with reference type performance."}]
